<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-Artin-Wedderburn-thm" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Artin-Wedderburn Theorem</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
        We are now in a position to state and proof Artin-Wedderburn theorem. As usual we assume that <m>R</m> is a commutative ring with unity and that <m>A</m> is an <m>R</m>-algebra.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <theorem xml:id="thm-Artin-Wedderburn">
    <title>Artin-Wedderburn</title>
    
    
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>A</m> be a right or a left semisimple <m>R</m>-algebra.
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        There exists natural numbers <m>n_1,n_2,\ldots,n_r</m> and <m>R</m>-division algebras <m>D_1,D_2,\ldots,D_r</m> such that
                        <men xml:id="eq-Artin-Wedderburn">
                            A\simeq M_{n_1}(D_1)\times M_{n_2}(D_2)\times\cdots\times M_{n_r}(D_r).
                        </men>
                        
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The pairs <m>(n_1,D_1),(n_2,D_2),\ldots,(n_r,D_r)</m> in <xref ref="eq-Artin-Wedderburn"/> are unique, up to an isomorphism.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Conversely, if <m>n_1,n_2,\ldots,n_r\in\N</m> and <m>D_1,D_2,\ldots,D_r</m> are division algebras over <m>R</m> then,
                        <me>
                            M_{n_1}(D_1)\times M_{n_2}(D_2)\times\cdots\times M_{n_r}(D_r)
                        </me>
                        is a right and a left semisimple <m>R</m>-algebra.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>
  <proof>
    <p>
        We prove the theorem for right <m>A</m>-modules. The left <m>A</m>-module analogue is left to the reader.
    </p>
    <p>
        Suppose that <m>A_A</m> is semisimple. As <m>A_A</m> is generated by <m>1_A</m> we have that <m>A_A\simeq\oplus_{i\in I}N_i</m>, where <m>N_i</m> are simple <m>A</m>-modules, i.e., <m>N_i</m> are minimal right ideals of <m>A</m>, and <m>|I|&lt;\infty</m> (see <xref ref="thm-semisimple-artinian-noetherian-modules"/>). Thus, <m>A_A\simeq M_1\oplus M_2\oplus\cdots\oplus M_r</m> with each <m>M_i</m> is a direct sum of <m>n_i</m> copies of a minimal right ideals <m>N_i</m>, and <m>N_i\not\simeq N_j</m> for <m>i\neq j</m>. We claim that <m>\Hom{A}{M_j}{M_i}=0</m> for <m>i\neq j</m>. Indeed, let <m>f\in\Hom{A}{M_j}{M_i}</m>. We have <m>M_j=\oplus_{k=1}^{n_j}N_{jk}</m> with <m>N_{jk}\simeq N_j</m> and <m>M_i=\oplus_{\ell=1}^{n_i}N_{i\ell}</m> with <m>N_{i\ell}\simeq N_i</m>. As <m>N_{jk}</m> is simple <m>A</m>-module, either <m>f|_{N_{jk}}=0</m> or <m>f|_{N_{jk}}(N_{jk})\simeq N_j\subseteq M_i</m>. Suppose that <m>0\neq f|_{N_{jk}}(N_{jk})\simeq N_j\subseteq M_i</m> By <xref ref="lem-step-in-charaterization-of-semisimple-modules"/>, there exists <m>J\subset\{1,2,\ldots,n_i\}</m> such that <me>M_i=f(N_{jk})\oplus\left(\oplus_{\ell\in J}N_{i\ell}\right)</me>.
        This implies that
        <me>
            M_i\big/\left(\oplus_{\ell\in J}N_{i\ell}\right)\simeq\bigoplus_{\ell\in\{1,2,\ldots,n_i\}\setminus J}N_{i\ell}\simeq f\left(N_{jk}\right).
        </me>
        Hence, <m>|\{1,2,\ldots,n_i\}\setminus J|=1</m> and thus, <m>N_j\simeq f\left(N_{jk}\right)\simeq N_i</m>, a contradiction. This shows that <m>f|_{N_{jk}}=0</m> for every <m>j</m>, and hence our claim is proved.
        Therefore, we obtain the following <m>R</m>-algebra isomorphisms.
        <md>
            <mrow> A\amp\simeq\End{A}{A_A}\amp </mrow>
            <mrow> \amp\simeq\End{A}{M_1\oplus M_2\oplus\cdots\oplus M_r}\amp</mrow>
            <mrow> \amp\simeq \End{A}{M_1}\times\End{A}{M_2}\times\cdots\times\End{A}{M_r}\amp\text{by }<xref ref="cor-endomorphism-algebra-as-diagonal-matrices"/></mrow>
            <mrow> \amp\simeq M_{n_1}\left(\End{A}{N_1}\right)\times M_{n_2}\left(\End{A}{N_2}\right)\times\cdots\times M_{n_r}\left(\End{A}{N_r}\right)\amp \text{by }<xref ref="cor-endomorphism-as-matrices"/> </mrow>
            <mrow> \amp\simeq M_{n_1}(D_1)\times M_{n_2}(D_2)\times\cdots\times M_{n_r}(D_r)\amp \text{by }<xref ref="lem-schur-lemma"/>,\, \End{A}{N_i}=D_i\text{ is division}</mrow>
        </md>
        
    </p>
    <p>
        Suppose that <m>A\simeq M_{k_1}(\Delta_1)\times M_{k_2}(\Delta_2)\times\cdots\times M_{k_s}(\Delta_s)</m>. For each <m>i</m>, as an <m>A</m>-module, <m>M_{k_i}(\Delta_i)</m> is a direct sum of minimal right ideal <m>P_i</m> of <m>A</m> with <m>P_i\subset M_{k_i}(\Delta_i)</m> (refer to <xref ref="ex-minimal-right-ideal-of-matrix-algebra"/> and <xref ref="lem-another-technical-lemma"/>). We put <m>A_i=M_{k_i}(\Delta_i)</m>. Furthermore, using <xref ref="lem-right-ideals-in-product-algebras"/> and <xref ref="endomorphisms-of-minimal-right-ideal-as-division-algebra"/> <m>\Delta_i\simeq\End{A}{P_i}\simeq\End{A_i}{P_i}</m>. As <m>A_i</m> are ideals and hence submodules containing <m>P_i</m>, using <xref ref="prop-equivalent-conditions-on-minimal-ideals-of-semisimple-algebras"/>, <m>P_i\not\simeq P_j</m> for <m>i\neq j</m>. By <xref ref="prop-uniqueness-of-direct-sum-in-semisimple-module"/>, we must have <m>r=s</m> and, after a possible reordering, <m>k_i=n_i</m> and <m>P_i\simeq N_i</m>. Hence, <m>D_i\simeq\End{A}{N_i}\simeq\End{A}{P_i}\simeq\Delta_i</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
        The last part of the theorem is left to the reader.
    </p>
  </proof>
  <note>
    <statement>
        The above <xref ref="thm-Artin-Wedderburn" /> asserts that the classes of semisimple left <m>A</m>-modules and semisimple right <m>A</m>-modules coincide.
    </statement>
  </note>
  <corollary xml:id="cor-simple-algebra-as-matrix-over-division-algebra">
    <statement>
        <p>
            A left or right Artinian algebra <m>A</m> is simple if and only if <m>A\simeq M_n(D)</m> for a natural number <m>n</m> and a division algebra <m>D</m>. In this case, <m>A</m> determines <m>n</m> uniquely and <m>D</m> is unique up to an isomorphism.
        </p>
    </statement>
  </corollary>
  <p>
    The following result require an observation that only finite-dimensional division algebra over an algebraically closed field <m>F</m> is <m>F</m>. This can be proved by replacing <m>\C</m> by <m>F</m> in <xref ref="lem-division-algebras-over-C"/>.
  </p>
  <corollary xml:id="cor-finite-dim-semisimple-algebra-over-algebraically-closed-field">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>F</m> be an algebraically closed field and let <m>A</m> be a finite-dimensional algebra over <m>F</m>. The algebra <m>A</m> is semisimple if and only if <m>A\simeq M_{n_1}(F)\times M_{n_2}(F)\times\cdots\times M_{n_r}(F)</m>, where <m>1\leq n_1\leq n_2\leq\cdots\leq n_r</m> are uniquely determined by the isomorphism type of <m>A</m>. Moreover, <m>A\simeq M_n(F)</m> if and only if <m>A</m> is simple. In this case <m>\dim_FA=n^2</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
  </corollary>
</section>